sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch12-05> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "When no constraint such as engineering design or natural selection forces simple behavior, the rules that operate in nature are effectively selected in no special way apart from tending to be structurally simple, so they behave like randomly chosen rules and are almost always universal when their behavior is not obviously simple.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Rules occurring in nature are typically not specially selected";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 12, “The Principle of Computational Equivalence”; section “The Content of the Principle” (from p. 719), p. 723. Scope: rules governing natural systems not constrained by engineering or natural selection"
}
